## Delta Lake — Complete Feature Reference

---

### 1. What Makes Delta Lake Different from Plain Parquet

| Feature | Parquet | Delta Lake |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ACID Transactions | No | Yes — via `_delta_log/` |
| Time Travel | No | Yes — `VERSION AS OF`, `TIMESTAMP AS OF` |
| Schema Enforcement | No (schema-on-read) | Yes — rejects bad writes |
| Schema Evolution | Manual | `ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMNS` or `mergeSchema` option |
| UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE | No | Yes — full DML support |
| Data Skipping | No | Yes — min/max stats per file per column |
| Streaming Source/Sink | Limited | Yes — exactly-once with checkpointing |
| Audit History | No | Yes — `DESCRIBE HISTORY` |

### 2. Delta Transaction Log (`_delta_log/`)

| Concept | Detail | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Location** | `_delta_log/` directory inside table directory | Every table has one |
| **Format** | JSON files (one per commit) + Parquet checkpoints every 10 commits | Checkpoint = snapshot for fast reads |
| **What it records** | Add file, remove file, metadata changes, protocol changes | Each commit is atomic |
| **Optimistic concurrency** | Writers check if conflicts exist at commit time | `ConcurrentAppendException` if conflict |
| **Conflict resolution** | File-level (not row-level) — two writers to different files = no conflict | This is why partitioning helps concurrency |

### 3. Time Travel

| Method | Syntax | Use Case |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **By version** | `SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 42` | Audit, debug, reproduce |
| **By timestamp** | `SELECT * FROM table TIMESTAMP AS OF '2026-08-01'` | Point-in-time reporting |
| **Restore** | `RESTORE TABLE table TO VERSION AS OF 42` | Undo bad writes |
| **History** | `DESCRIBE HISTORY table` | See all commits with timestamps, operations, users |
| **Retention** | Default 30 days for data files | Controlled by `delta.logRetentionDuration` (log) and `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` (data) |

### 4. Table Maintenance Commands

| Command | What It Does | When to Run | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **OPTIMIZE** | Compacts small files into larger ones (\~1 GB target) | After many small writes (streaming, frequent appends) | Does NOT delete old files — just adds new compacted ones |
| **OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY** | Compacts + co-locates data by specified columns | When queries filter on specific non-partition columns | Max 4 columns recommended; diminishing returns after |
| **VACUUM** | Deletes files older than retention period that are no longer referenced | Weekly or after OPTIMIZE | Default retention = 7 days. **Cannot undo VACUUM** — breaks time travel for vacuumed versions |
| **ANALYZE TABLE** | Computes column-level statistics for the optimizer | After large data changes; before expensive queries | Critical for correct join strategy selection |
| **DESCRIBE DETAIL** | Shows file count, size, partition columns, table properties | Diagnostic — check table health | Key fields: `numFiles`, `sizeInBytes`, `partitionColumns` |
| **DESCRIBE HISTORY** | Shows commit log with timestamps, operations, metrics | Debugging, auditing | Shows `operationMetrics` (rows written, files added) |

### 5. Deletion Vectors

| Aspect | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **What** | A bitmap that marks rows as "deleted" within a Parquet file without rewriting the file |
| **Why** | DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE no longer need to rewrite entire files — just append a deletion vector |
| **Performance** | 2-10x faster DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE on large tables |
| **Enable** | `ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableDeletionVectors' = true)` |
| **Default** | Enabled by default on new tables (DBR 14.0+) |
| **Read impact** | Readers apply the deletion vector as a filter — slight overhead, but much less than rewriting |
| **Exam tip** | Deletion vectors make MERGE cheaper because only the deletion bitmap is written, not new Parquet files |

### 6. Change Data Feed (CDF)

| Aspect | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **What** | Records row-level changes (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) for downstream consumers |
| **Enable** | `ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableChangeDataFeed' = true)` |
| **Query** | `SELECT * FROM table_changes('my_table', 2)` — changes since version 2 |
| **Use case** | Incremental ETL: downstream tables read only changed rows instead of full table |
| **Storage** | Changes stored in `_change_data/` directory inside the table |
| **Exam tip** | CDF is the foundation for incremental MERGE patterns — enables "process only what changed" |

### 7. Liquid Clustering vs Partitioning vs Z-Order

| Aspect | PARTITION BY | Z-ORDER BY | CLUSTER BY (Liquid) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Set at** | CREATE TABLE time only | On-demand with OPTIMIZE | CREATE TABLE or ALTER TABLE anytime |
| **Change columns** | Cannot change — rebuild required | Can change anytime | Can change with ALTER TABLE |
| **How it works** | Physical directories per value | Interleaved sort within files | Hilbert-curve layout within files |
| **Best for** | Low-cardinality (date, region) | Ad-hoc optimization, 1-4 columns | Any cardinality, up to 4 columns |
| **Small file problem** | Yes — high cardinality = millions of tiny files | No | No |
| **Incremental** | N/A | Must re-run OPTIMIZE on all data | OPTIMIZE processes only new/changed files |
| **Exam tip** | Legacy — use Liquid Clustering for new tables | Being replaced by Liquid Clustering | **Recommended approach** for all new Delta tables |

 
## Unity Catalog — Governance & Security

---

### 1. Hierarchy

```
Metastore (1 per region — shared across workspaces)
 └── Catalog (top-level namespace — like a database in traditional RDBMS)
      └── Schema (like a schema/database — groups related objects)
           ├── Tables (managed or external)
           ├── Views (standard, materialized, dynamic)
           ├── Volumes (managed or external file storage)
           ├── Functions (SQL UDFs, Python UDFs)
           └── Models (ML models registered via MLflow)
```

### 2. Object Types

| Object | Managed | External | Key Difference |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Table** | Data in UC-managed storage; DROP deletes data | Data at your LOCATION; DROP removes metadata only | Managed = simpler; External = portable |
| **Volume** | Files in UC-managed storage | Files at your LOCATION | Use for non-tabular files (images, CSVs, JARs) |
| **View** | Always virtual (no storage) | N/A | `CREATE VIEW` — query alias with access control |
| **Dynamic View** | Row/column filtering based on user | N/A | `CASE WHEN is_member('group') THEN col ELSE 'REDACTED' END` |
| **Materialized View** | Pre-computed + auto-refreshed | N/A | Speeds up dashboards and repeated queries |

### 3. Access Control (Privileges)

| Privilege | Applies To | What It Allows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ALL PRIVILEGES` | Any securable | Full access (owner-level) |
| `SELECT` | Table, View | Read data |
| `MODIFY` | Table | INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE |
| `CREATE TABLE` | Schema | Create new tables |
| `CREATE SCHEMA` | Catalog | Create new schemas |
| `CREATE CATALOG` | Metastore | Create new catalogs |
| `USE CATALOG` | Catalog | Access the catalog (required to see schemas) |
| `USE SCHEMA` | Schema | Access the schema (required to see tables) |
| `EXECUTE` | Function | Run a UDF |
| `READ VOLUME` | Volume | Read files from a volume |
| `WRITE VOLUME` | Volume | Write files to a volume |

**Exam-critical syntax:**
```sql
GRANT SELECT ON TABLE catalog.schema.table TO `user@example.com`;
GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG my_catalog TO `group_name`;
GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA my_catalog.my_schema TO `group_name`;
GRANT CREATE TABLE ON SCHEMA my_catalog.my_schema TO `group_name`;
REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE catalog.schema.table FROM `user@example.com`;
SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE catalog.schema.table;
SHOW GRANTS `user@example.com`;
```

### 4. Row-Level & Column-Level Security

| Type | How | Syntax |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Row Filter** | Function that returns TRUE/FALSE per row | `ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER func ON (col1, col2)` |
| **Column Mask** | Function that transforms a column value per user/group | `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_ssn` |
| **Dynamic View** | CASE WHEN inside a VIEW definition | `CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT CASE WHEN is_member('hr') THEN salary ELSE NULL END ...` |

### 5. Data Lineage

| Feature | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **What** | Automatic tracking of data flow: which tables/columns are read/written by which notebooks/jobs |
| **Granularity** | Table-level and column-level |
| **Where to see** | Catalog Explorer → Table → Lineage tab |
| **Use case** | Impact analysis ("if I change this column, what downstream tables break?") |
| **Exam tip** | Lineage is automatic — no configuration needed. Works for SQL, Python, Scala, R |

### 6. Delta Sharing

| Concept | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **What** | Open protocol for secure cross-organization data sharing |
| **Types** | Open sharing (any client, token-based) and Databricks-to-Databricks (UC-level) |
| **Share** | A named container of tables you want to share: `CREATE SHARE my_share` |
| **Recipient** | Who you share with: `CREATE RECIPIENT partner_corp` |
| **Grant** | `GRANT SELECT ON SHARE my_share TO RECIPIENT partner_corp` |
| **Add tables** | `ALTER SHARE my_share ADD TABLE catalog.schema.table` |
| **Consumer** | Creates a catalog from the share: `CREATE CATALOG partner_data USING SHARE provider.share_name` |
| **Exam tip** | Data is NOT copied — consumers query it in place. Provider controls access |

 
## Data Engineering — ETL, Pipelines, Auto Loader & SDP

---

### 1. Auto Loader (cloudFiles)

| Aspect | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **What** | Incrementally and efficiently processes new files as they arrive in cloud storage |
| **How** | Uses file notification (event-based) or directory listing to detect new files |
| **Format** | `spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles')` |
| **Key option** | `.option('cloudFiles.format', 'json')` — supports JSON, CSV, Parquet, Avro, ORC, text, binary |
| **Schema inference** | Automatic with `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes = true` |
| **Schema evolution** | `cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode = addNewColumns` — auto-adds new columns |
| **Rescue column** | `_rescued_data` — captures data that doesn't match the schema instead of failing |
| **Checkpoint** | Tracks which files have been processed — exactly-once semantics |

**Two modes:**

| Mode | How | When to Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Directory Listing** | Lists the directory periodically | < 10K files/day, simpler setup |
| **File Notification** | Cloud events (Azure Event Grid, AWS SNS/SQS) | > 10K files/day, lower latency |

**Exam-critical pattern:**
```python
df = (spark.readStream
  .format('cloudFiles')
  .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json')
  .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true')
  .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/path/to/schema')
  .load('/path/to/source/')
)
df.writeStream.option('checkpointLocation', '/path/to/checkpoint').table('bronze.events')
```

### 2. Spark Declarative Pipelines (SDP, formerly DLT)

| Concept | Detail | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **What** | Declarative ETL framework — you define WHAT, Spark handles HOW | You write `CREATE STREAMING TABLE` or `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW` |
| **Streaming Table** | Append-only table fed by a streaming source | Use for raw/bronze data, CDC |
| **Materialized View** | Auto-refreshed query result | Use for aggregations, gold layer |
| **Expectations** | Data quality constraints with actions | `CONSTRAINT valid_id EXPECT (id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW` |
| **Pipeline modes** | Triggered (batch) or Continuous (streaming) | Triggered for cost savings; Continuous for low latency |
| **Development vs Production** | Dev mode = reuses cluster, fast iteration; Prod mode = new cluster per run | Always use Production for scheduled jobs |

**Expectations actions:**

| Action | Behavior | When to Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ON VIOLATION WARN` | Logs the violation, keeps the row | Monitoring — see how many bad records |
| `ON VIOLATION DROP ROW` | Silently drops the row | Filter out bad data |
| `ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE` | Fails the entire pipeline | Critical data — no bad records allowed |

### 3. Structured Streaming Concepts

| Concept | Detail | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Trigger** | How often to process: `availableNow` (all pending, then stop), `processingTime` (interval), continuous | `availableNow` = batch-like but incremental |
| **Checkpoint** | Stores progress (which files/offsets processed) | Required for exactly-once; cannot change query between restarts without resetting |
| **Watermark** | Threshold for late data: `withWatermark('event_time', '10 minutes')` | Data arriving after watermark is dropped |
| **Output modes** | Append (new rows only), Complete (full result), Update (changed rows) | Append is most common; Complete for aggregations |

### 4. Medallion Architecture Exam Reference

| Layer | Purpose | Table Type | Data Quality | Consumers |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Bronze** | Raw ingestion, append-only | Streaming Table | Minimal — schema enforcement only | Silver layer |
| **Silver** | Cleaned, conformed, deduplicated | Streaming Table or MV | High — expectations, null handling, type casting | Gold layer, data science |
| **Gold** | Business-ready aggregations | Materialized View | Highest — pre-aggregated, validated | Dashboards, reports, APIs |

### 5. COPY INTO vs Auto Loader

| Aspect | COPY INTO | Auto Loader |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Type** | SQL command (batch) | Streaming source |
| **Idempotent** | Yes — tracks loaded files | Yes — via checkpoint |
| **Scale** | Millions of files (slower at very high volume) | Billions of files (file notification scales better) |
| **Schema evolution** | Manual | Automatic with rescue column |
| **Use case** | One-time loads, simple ETL | Production incremental ingestion |
| **Exam tip** | Good for ad-hoc; Auto Loader for production pipelines |

 
## Databricks SQL & Warehouses

---

### 1. SQL Warehouse Types

| Type | Startup | Scaling | Best For | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Serverless** | Seconds | Instant auto-scale | Most SQL workloads | Recommended default — no infra to manage |
| **Pro** | Minutes | Auto-scale (slower) | Advanced SQL features needed | Has Photon, serverless-like features |
| **Classic** | Minutes | Auto-scale (slower) | Legacy/cost-sensitive | Being deprecated in favor of Serverless |

### 2. Warehouse Sizing

| Size | Clusters (min) | Concurrent Queries | Monthly Cost (approx) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2X-Small | 1 | Low | Lowest |
| X-Small | 1 | Low-Medium | Low |
| Small | 1 | Medium | Medium |
| Medium | 1 | Medium-High | High |
| Large | 1 | High | Higher |
| X-Large+ | 1+ | Very High | Highest |

**Scaling = adding more clusters** (not bigger clusters). Each cluster handles a set of concurrent queries. `Max clusters = 2` means up to 2 identical clusters for double throughput.

### 3. Query Execution Features

| Feature | What It Does | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Photon** | C++ vectorized engine — 2-8x faster for SQL | Enabled by default on SQL Warehouses |
| **Result Caching** | Identical queries return cached results instantly | Cache invalidated when underlying data changes |
| **Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)** | Optimizes plan at runtime based on actual data | Auto-coalesces partitions, handles skew |
| **Predictive I/O** | ML model predicts which files to read | Reduces unnecessary file scans |
| **Query Profile** | Visual DAG showing operators, time, rows, spill | Primary debugging tool for slow queries |

### 4. SQL Objects You Can Create

| Object | Syntax | Persisted? | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Table** | `CREATE TABLE t (...)` | Yes — data + metadata | Default: managed Delta table |
| **External Table** | `CREATE TABLE t (...) LOCATION 'path'` | Metadata only — data at location | DROP removes metadata only |
| **View** | `CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT ...` | Metadata only — no data | Re-executes query each time |
| **Materialized View** | `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW mv AS SELECT ...` | Yes — pre-computed result | Auto-refreshes; faster than views |
| **Streaming Table** | `CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE st AS SELECT ...` | Yes — incrementally updated | Append-only from streaming sources |
| **Temporary View** | `CREATE TEMP VIEW tv AS SELECT ...` | Session only — gone after disconnect | Use for intermediate results |
| **Function (SQL UDF)** | `CREATE FUNCTION f(x INT) RETURNS INT RETURN x * 2` | Yes — stored in UC | Reusable across queries |
| **Volume** | `CREATE VOLUME v` | Yes — file container | Non-tabular file storage in UC |

### 5. Key SQL Patterns for Exam

| Pattern | Syntax | When |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **MERGE (Upsert)** | `MERGE INTO target USING source ON ... WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT` | Incremental loads, SCD Type 1 |
| **COPY INTO** | `COPY INTO table FROM 'path' FILEFORMAT = CSV` | One-time or periodic file ingestion |
| **INSERT OVERWRITE** | `INSERT OVERWRITE table SELECT ...` | Replace all data (or partition) |
| **CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE** | `CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE t AS SELECT ...` | Atomic table replacement |
| **CTAS** | `CREATE TABLE t AS SELECT ...` | Create + populate in one step |
| **DEEP CLONE** | `CREATE TABLE t DEEP CLONE source` | Full copy (data + metadata) |
| **SHALLOW CLONE** | `CREATE TABLE t SHALLOW CLONE source` | Metadata-only copy (references same files) |

### 6. Dashboards (Lakeview)

| Feature | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **What** | Native BI dashboards built from SQL queries |
| **Types** | Bar, line, area, pie, scatter, heatmap, histogram, table, counter |
| **Parameters** | Dynamic filters that apply across widgets: `:param_name` syntax |
| **Refresh** | Manual or scheduled (e.g., every 1 hour) |
| **Sharing** | Viewer/Editor/Owner permissions via workspace |
| **Exam tip** | Dashboards run on SQL Warehouses — no cluster needed |
 
## Lakeflow Jobs & Compute Types

---

### 1. Lakeflow Jobs (formerly Workflows)

| Concept | Detail | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Job** | A scheduled execution of one or more tasks | Top-level orchestration unit |
| **Task** | A single unit of work: notebook, SQL, Python script, SDP pipeline, JAR, dbt | Building block of a job |
| **Task Dependencies** | Tasks can depend on other tasks — creates a DAG | Downstream tasks wait for upstream to succeed |
| **Trigger types** | Manual, Scheduled (cron), File arrival, Continuous, Table update | Table update = run when a Delta table changes |
| **Retry policy** | Per-task: max retries + retry on timeout | Use for transient failures (network, spot preemption) |
| **Parameters** | Pass values from job to task or between tasks | `{{job.parameter_name}}` syntax |
| **Notifications** | Email/webhook on start, success, failure | Best practice: always set failure notifications |

### 2. Task Types

| Task Type | What It Runs | Use Case |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Notebook** | Databricks notebook (Python/SQL/Scala/R) | Most common — ETL, ML, analysis |
| **SQL** | SQL query or dashboard refresh | Scheduled queries, report refresh |
| **Python Script** | .py file from workspace or Git | Lightweight Python jobs |
| **SDP Pipeline** | Spark Declarative Pipeline | Managed ETL with quality expectations |
| **JAR** | Java/Scala JAR file | Legacy Spark jobs |
| **dbt** | dbt project | dbt SQL transformations |
| **If/else condition** | Conditional branching based on task results | "Run task B only if task A output > 100" |
| **For each** | Loop over a list, running subtasks per item | Process multiple files/tables in parallel |

### 3. Compute Types — Complete Comparison

| Aspect | SQL Warehouse (Serverless) | All-Purpose Cluster | Job Cluster | Serverless Compute |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Languages** | SQL only | Python, SQL, Scala, R | Python, SQL, Scala, R | Python, SQL |
| **Startup** | Seconds | 5-10 minutes | 5-10 minutes | Seconds |
| **Lifecycle** | Auto-stop (configurable) | Manual or auto-stop | Created per job run, terminated after | Auto-managed |
| **Shared by** | Multiple users/queries | Multiple users (interactive) | Single job run only | Single notebook/job |
| **Photon** | Always on | Optional (enable at cluster creation) | Optional | Always on |
| **Cost model** | DBU/hr while running | DBU/hr while cluster is up | DBU/hr while job runs | DBU/hr while running |
| **Scaling** | Auto (add warehouse clusters) | Auto (add worker nodes) | Fixed or auto | Auto |
| **Best for** | SQL analytics, dashboards, BI | Interactive development, ML experimentation | Production ETL, scheduled jobs | Notebooks (Python/SQL) |
| **Exam tip** | Default choice for SQL workloads | Most expensive (stays up) | Most cost-effective for batch | Newest option — no cluster management |

### 4. Cluster Configuration Exam Points

| Setting | What It Does | Exam Tip |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Spot/Preemptible instances** | Use discounted cloud VMs (can be reclaimed) | Use for workers (not driver) — saves 30-70% |
| **Auto-scaling** | Add/remove workers based on load | Set min/max workers; good for variable workloads |
| **Auto-termination** | Shut down after idle period | Always enable — prevents forgotten clusters |
| **Cluster policies** | Admin-defined rules limiting cluster configs | Enforce cost controls: max workers, instance types, auto-stop |
| **Init scripts** | Shell scripts that run on cluster startup | Install custom libraries, configure environments |
| **Spark config** | Key-value Spark settings | `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`, `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` |
| **Instance pools** | Pre-warmed VMs ready to attach | Reduces cluster startup time to \~2 minutes |

### 5. When to Use Which Compute

| Scenario | Best Compute | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Analyst running ad-hoc SQL | Serverless SQL Warehouse | Instant start, pay-per-query, SQL-optimized |
| Dashboard serving 100 users | Serverless SQL Warehouse (Medium+) | Auto-scales with concurrent users |
| Data engineer developing in notebook | Serverless Compute or All-Purpose | Interactive, supports Python |
| Nightly ETL pipeline | Job Cluster | Ephemeral — created and destroyed per run |
| ML model training (GPU) | All-Purpose with ML Runtime | Needs GPU + ML libraries |
| Streaming pipeline (24/7) | Job Cluster (always-on) | Continuous, dedicated resources |
| Quick one-off query | Serverless SQL Warehouse | Starts in seconds |

 
## MLflow & Machine Learning on Databricks

